// Bone landmarks as the osteology slides give them: [pattern, landmarks, extra].
window.BLAND=[["^humerus$", "Proximal end: humeral head, greater tuberosity (posterior), lesser tuberosity (anterior), intertubercular sulcus (bicipital groove), deltoid tuberosity. Distal end: capitulum, trochlea, olecranon fossa, medial and lateral supracondylar ridges, medial epicondyle, lateral epicondyle", ""], ["^scapula$", "Supraspinous fossa, infraspinous fossa, spine of scapula, acromion process, coracoid process, medial border, lateral border, inferior angle, glenoid fossa", "The glenoid fossa is deepened by the glenoid labrum, a narrow rim of fibrocartilage around the edge of the fossa. The glenohumeral joint is a spheroid (shallow ball and socket) joint: the most movable joint in the body, with mobility at the expense of stability."], ["^clavicle$", "Acromion end, sternoclavicular end, conoid tubercle", "Forms the only bony attachment to the axial skeleton, at the SC joint. \"S\"-shaped bone: convex along the medial 2/3 and concave along the distal 1/3. Acts as a \"strut\" to allow the upper extremity to swing clear of the trunk."], ["^ulna$", "Olecranon process, trochlear notch/fossa, coronoid process, ulnar tuberosity, radial notch, styloid process", "In pronation and supination the ulna stays fixed while the radius rotates around it. The annular ligament wraps from anterior to posterior around the neck of the radius and connects to the radial notch on the ulna."], ["^radius$", "Radial head, radial tuberosity, styloid process", "Rotates medially or laterally around the ulna in pronation and supination; its concave shape gives clearance for soft tissue and the radial tuberosity. The distal radioulnar joint is the ulnar head against the ulnar notch of the radius."], ["^scaphoid$", "", "Also called the navicular: the most commonly fractured bone. Proximal row."], ["^lunate$", "", "Proximal row."], ["^triquetral$", "", "Also called the triquetrum. Proximal row."], ["^pisiform$", "", "A sesamoid bone in the tendon of flexor carpi ulnaris. Proximal row."], ["^trapezium$", "", "Distal row. 1st CMC joint (metacarpal 1 with trapezium) is a sellar (saddle) joint."], ["^trapezoid$", "", "Distal row. 2nd CMC joint: metacarpal 2 with trapezoid."], ["^capitate$", "", "Distal row. 3rd CMC joint: metacarpal 3 with capitate."], ["^hamate$", "Hook of hamate", "Distal row. 4th CMC joint: metacarpal 4 with hamate."], ["^first metacarpal", "", "Two small sesamoid bones over the palmar aspect of its distal end improve the mechanical line of pull for flexor pollicis brevis, abductor pollicis brevis and adductor pollicis."], ["metacarpal", "", "CMC joints: 1st with the trapezium, 2nd with the trapezoid, 3rd with the capitate, 4th with the hamate."], ["phalanx of (thumb|index|middle finger|ring finger|little finger)", "Proximal, middle and distal phalanges", "Joints: MCP between metacarpal and proximal phalanx; IP joints, proximal (PIP) and distal (DIP), between the phalanges."], ["cervical vertebra$|^seventh cervical", "Vertebral body, neural arch, pedicles, lamina, transverse process, foramen transversarium, vertebral notch, bifid spinous process, superior and inferior articular processes, vertebral foramen", "Facet joints are angled about 45° from the transverse plane: they allow flexion and extension but limit rotation and lateral flexion. The transverse foramina carry the vertebral artery, which takes blood from the heart to the brain. The upper and lower cervical spine is one of the most mobile regions."], ["^atlas$", "Anterior and posterior tubercles, superior articular surfaces", "Atlanto-occipital (AO) joint, between the occipital condyles and the atlas: flexion/extension 15–20°, lateral flexion 10°."], ["^axis$", "Odontoid process (dens)", "Atlanto-axial (AA) joint is a pivot joint: rotation 75°, flexion/extension 10°, lateral flexion 25°."], ["thoracic vertebra$", "Vertebral body, superior and inferior costal facets, pedicles, lamina, transverse process, costotransverse facet, vertebral notch, spinous process, superior and inferior articular processes", "Facet joints are angled 60° to the transverse plane and 20° to the frontal plane, and the ribs restrict motion, so the thoracic region is the least mobile. Ribs attach at the costotransverse and costovertebral joints."], ["lumbar vertebra$", "Vertebral body, pedicles, lamina, transverse process, vertebral notch, spinous process, superior and inferior articular processes", "Facet joints are angled 90° to both the frontal and transverse planes: mainly trunk flexion and extension, with limited rotation and side bending. L5–S1 is one of the most mobile segments."], ["^sacrum$", "Base (superior surface of S1), superior articular processes, four pairs of sacral foramina", "Large, triangular wedge-shaped bone of five fused vertebrae. Its superior articular processes articulate with L5, forming the lumbosacral angle; it forms the back of the bony pelvis. The sacroiliac joints are strong synovial joints whose irregular surfaces partly interlock."], ["^hip bone$", "Iliac crest, anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS), posterior superior iliac spine (PSIS), posterior inferior iliac spine (PIIS), ischial tuberosity, sciatic notch, obturator foramen, acetabulum", "Innominate bone: three fused bones (ilium, ischium, pubis). Hip joint: multiaxial ball and socket synovial joint between the femoral head and the acetabulum, deepened by the fibrocartilaginous acetabular labrum. Pubic symphysis: cartilaginous joint with a fibrocartilaginous interpubic disc."], ["^femur$", "Greater trochanter, lesser trochanter, medial and lateral condyles, medial and lateral epicondyles, intercondylar notch, patellar surface (groove)", "In the transverse plane the femoral head and shaft form an angle of 12–15°. Retroversion (under 12°) gives a toed-out gait; anteversion (over 15°) gives a toe-in, pigeon-toed gait. Bursae: trochanteric, iliopsoas, ischial."], ["^patella$", "", "Increases the efficiency of the quadriceps by increasing its lever arm (type I lever: the patella is the fulcrum). It tracks vertically in the femoral groove; contact with the groove is largest at 90° of knee flexion. Chondromalacia patellae is softening of its articular cartilage."], ["^tibia$", "Medial and lateral condyles, tibial tuberosity, medial malleolus", "Medial, weight-bearing bone. Knee: extension to 0° (beyond is genu recurvatum), flexion to 140–160°. Menisci: medial is O-shaped, lateral is C-shaped. ACL and PCL cross, named for their tibial attachments; MCL resists genu valgum, LCL resists genu varum. Deltoid ligament runs from the medial malleolus to the talus, navicular and calcaneus and prevents valgus stress."], ["^fibula$", "Head of fibula, lateral malleolus", "Located laterally. Lateral ankle ligaments: anterior talofibular (tight in plantar flexion, resists inversion), posterior talofibular (strongest) and calcaneofibular (tight in extreme dorsiflexion). Lateral sprains are 85% of ankle sprains."], ["^calcaneus$", "", "Tarsal bone. The Achilles tendon (gastrocnemius and soleus) inserts on it; the plantar fascia starts at the medial calcaneal tuberosity and runs to the bases of the digits, supporting the medial longitudinal arch."], ["^talus$", "", "Tarsal bone. The deltoid ligament fans out to it from the medial malleolus."], ["^navicular bone of foot$", "Tuberosity of the navicular", "Tarsal bone. Tibialis posterior inserts on its tuberosity."], ["^cuboid bone$", "", "Tarsal bone."], ["cuneiform bone$", "", "Tarsal bone (cuneiforms: medial, intermediate, lateral)."], ["^fifth metatarsal", "Styloid process of the base", "Fibularis brevis inserts on the styloid process."], ["metatarsal", "", "Metatarsals 1–5 lie between the tarsals and the phalanges."], ["phalanx of .*toe", "Proximal, middle and distal phalanges (the great toe has no middle phalanx)", ""]].map(x=>[new RegExp(x[0]),x[1],x[2]]);
// Slide facts for discs, tendons and ligaments: [pattern, text].
window.TFACTS=[["intervertebral disk", "22 discs, named for the vertebrae they sit between. High water content (about 90%), which decreases with age. Nucleus pulposus: gelatinous material that resists compression. Annulus fibrosus: about 90 concentric bands of collagen, fibers at 30° to each other, resistant to rotational stress. Discs bind adjacent vertebrae, absorb shock, and allow and resist motion. Intradiscal pressure is lowest lying supine and highest sitting slumped (Nachemson, 1975)."], ["calcaneal tendon", "Achilles tendon: the gastrocnemius aponeurosis and the deep tendon of the soleus unite to form it, inserting on the calcaneus."], ["long plantar ligament", "Ligaments give the foot its static stability: the long plantar ligament, the spring (plantar calcaneonavicular) ligament, and the plantar fascia."], ["interosseous membrane of leg", "The bones and tough fascial sheaths divide the lower leg into four compartments (anterior, lateral, superficial posterior, deep posterior), each with its own muscles, nerves and blood supply."], ["inguinal ligament", "Internal oblique and transversus abdominis start partly on it; external oblique inserts on it."]].map(x=>[new RegExp(x[0]),x[1]]);
